<DocsLayout title="MCP Server" description="MCP integration for AI agents">
  <h1>MCP Server</h1>

  <p>MCP is provided by <code>relay-core-probe</code> (npm package <code>@relay-core/mcp</code>), which is a <strong>bridge</strong>: it forwards MCP messages between stdin/stdout and the daemon's <code>/mcp</code> endpoint.</p>

  <p><strong>Connecting an MCP client starts no engine.</strong> The proxy, the flow history, the rules and the intercept queue all belong to the daemon, so the CLI, your editor and the Web UI see one state rather than one each. The daemon also serves the MCP endpoint over HTTP, so a client that can be configured with a remote MCP server can skip the bridge entirely.</p>

  <h2>Prerequisites</h2>

  <pre><code>npx @relay-core/cli ca generate
npx @relay-core/cli ca install   # macOS helper; see ca status on other platforms
npx @relay-core/cli start        # daemon + proxy (optional on first run: the bridge starts one)</code></pre>

  <h2>Configure your MCP client</h2>

  <p>In Cursor, Claude Desktop, or any MCP client:</p>
  <pre><code>&#123;
  "mcpServers": &#123;
    "relay-core": &#123;
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "@relay-core/mcp"]
    &#125;
  &#125;
&#125;</code></pre>

  <p>When no daemon is running, the bridge starts one (detached, so closing your editor does not take it with it). It does <strong>not</strong> start a proxy: that stays an explicit command — <code>relay-core-cli start</code> / <code>stop</code>, or the <code>proxy_start</code> / <code>proxy_stop</code> tools. Set <code>RELAY_MCP_NO_AUTOSTART=1</code>, or <code>[client] autostart_daemon = false</code> in the config file, to require starting the daemon yourself.</p>

  <h2>Bridge flags</h2>

  <p>The bridge accepts a small set of flags and env vars:</p>

  <table>
    <tr><th>Flag</th><th>Env</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr>
    <tr><td>&mdash;</td><td><code>RELAY_DATA_DIR</code></td><td><code>~/.relay-core</code></td><td>Which daemon to attach to</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>--mcp-url &lt;URL&gt;</code></td><td><code>RELAY_MCP_URL</code></td><td>discovered</td><td>Skip discovery and use this MCP endpoint</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>--token &lt;T&gt;</code></td><td><code>RELAY_MCP_TOKEN</code></td><td>from manifest</td><td>Bearer token for that endpoint</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>--no-autostart</code></td><td><code>RELAY_MCP_NO_AUTOSTART=1</code></td><td>auto-start on</td><td>Refuse to start a daemon</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h3>Lifecycle</h3>

  <p><code>proxy_status</code> reports whether the proxy is running, on which port, for how long, and why it failed if it did. <code>proxy_start</code> starts it — idempotent, and a taken port answers <code>start_failed</code> rather than success for a proxy that never bound. <code>proxy_stop</code> stops the proxy and keeps the daemon and its history. All three carry read-only / destructive / idempotent annotations and are listed first.</p>

  <p>When no proxy is running, traffic tools answer a structured <code>proxy_not_running</code> error (in the error's <code>data.code</code>) instead of an empty list that reads as "no traffic". History that was already captured is still returned while the proxy is stopped.</p>

  <h2>Tools</h2>

  <h3>Observe</h3>

  <div class="endpoint">
    <span class="method method-post">search_flows</span>
  </div>
  <p>Search and filter captured flows.</p>
  <pre><code>&#123;
  "filter": "method:GET host:*.example.com",
  "limit": 10
&#125;</code></pre>

  <div class="endpoint">
    <span class="method method-post">get_flow</span>
  </div>
  <p>Get detailed flow information.</p>
  <pre><code>&#123;
  "flow_id": "fld_01HX5K9P3Q8Y2Z7"
&#125;</code></pre>

  <div class="endpoint">
    <span class="method method-post">get_metrics</span>
  </div>
  <p>Get current proxy metrics.</p>

  <h3>Control</h3>

  <div class="endpoint">
    <span class="method method-post">set_intercept</span>
  </div>
  <p>Create an interception breakpoint.</p>
  <pre><code>&#123;
  "filter": &#123;
    "method": "POST",
    "host": "api.example.com"
  &#125;,
  "pause_at": "request"
&#125;</code></pre>

  <div class="endpoint">
    <span class="method method-post">get_pending_intercepts</span>
  </div>
  <p>List the currently pending intercepts (HTTP and WebSocket).</p>

  <div class="endpoint">
    <span class="method method-post">resume_flow</span>
  </div>
  <p>Resume a paused flow.</p>
  <pre><code>&#123;
  "flow_id": "fld_01HX5K9P3Q8Y2Z7",
  "action": "resume",  // or "drop"
  "modifications": &#123;
    "status_code": 418,
    "response_body": "I'm a teapot"
  &#125;
&#125;</code></pre>

  <h3>Rules</h3>

  <div class="endpoint">
    <span class="method method-post">set_rule</span>
  </div>
  <p>Create or update a rule.</p>
  <pre><code>&#123;
  "name": "Log API requests",
  "filter": &#123; "path": "/api/*" &#125;,
  "action": &#123; "type": "log" &#125;
&#125;</code></pre>

  <div class="endpoint">
    <span class="method method-post">delete_rule</span>
  </div>
  <p>Delete a rule.</p>
  <pre><code>&#123;
  "rule_id": "rul_01HX5K9P3Q8Y2Z7"
&#125;</code></pre>

  <div class="endpoint">
    <span class="method method-post">mock_url</span>
  </div>
  <p>Create a one-off <code>MockResponse</code> rule.</p>
  <pre><code>&#123;
  "url_pattern": "api.example.com/v1/health",
  "status": 200,
  "body": "&#123;\"ok\":true&#125;",
  "content_type": "application/json"
&#125;</code></pre>

  <h3>Scripting</h3>

  <div class="endpoint">
    <span class="method method-post">set_script</span>
  </div>
  <p>Hot-load a Deno script into the running engine.</p>
  <pre><code>&#123;
  "script": "// deno-fmt-ignore\nexport default &#123; onRequest(ctx) &#123; /* ... */ &#125; &#125;"
&#125;</code></pre>

  <h3>Analyze</h3>

  <div class="endpoint">
    <span class="method method-post">export_har</span>
  </div>
  <p>Export flows as HAR format.</p>
  <pre><code>&#123;
  "filter": "host:example.com",
  "format": "har"
&#125;</code></pre>

  <div class="endpoint">
    <span class="method method-post">replay_flow</span>
  </div>
  <p>Replay a captured flow.</p>
  <pre><code>&#123;
  "flow_id": "fld_01HX5K9P3Q8Y2Z7",
  "modifications": &#123;
    "headers": &#123; "x-test": "value" &#125;
  &#125;
&#125;</code></pre>

  <h3>Policy</h3>

  <div class="endpoint">
    <span class="method method-post">get_policy</span>
  </div>
  <p>Get current interception policy, including the redaction block.</p>

  <div class="endpoint">
    <span class="method method-post">update_policy</span>
  </div>
  <p>Replace the entire policy.</p>

  <div class="endpoint">
    <span class="method method-post">patch_policy</span>
  </div>
  <p>Apply a partial update — only the supplied keys are touched. Accepts the same <code>&#123; redaction?, upstream? &#125;</code> shape as the REST API.</p>
  <pre><code>&#123;
  "redaction": &#123;
    "enabled": true,
    "redact_bodies": true
  &#125;
&#125;</code></pre>

  <h2>Result shape</h2>

  <p>Every tool returns <code>structuredContent</code> (typed fields an agent reads directly, with a declared <code>outputSchema</code> where the shape is stable) plus the same JSON as text, so old and new clients cannot disagree about what a call returned. Writes return <code>&#123; ok, message, ... &#125;</code>: success is a field, not a phrase to match. <code>search_flows</code> returns <code>&#123; count, flows &#125;</code>. Tool contract version: <strong>2</strong>.</p>

  <h2>Resources</h2>

  <p>MCP also exposes read-only resources:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>flows://</code> — Recent flows</li>
    <li><code>rules://</code> — Active rules</li>
    <li><code>proxy://status</code> — Proxy status and metrics</li>
    <li><code>audit://recent</code> — Recent audit events</li>
    <li><code>ca://install</code> — CA install guide with default paths</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Subscriptions</h3>
  <p>The server declares the resources-subscribe capability and accepts a subscription for exactly the
    URIs it pushes updates for — <code>flows://</code>, <code>flows://&#123;id&#125;</code>,
    <code>rules://</code>, <code>proxy://status</code> and <code>audit://recent</code> — so a client that
    subscribes is a client that will actually be notified. Any other URI is refused with an error rather
    than silently accepted, because a subscription that can never produce an update only tells a client
    to wait. <code>unsubscribe</code> is accepted; the server broadcasts to whoever is connected and does
    not keep per-subscriber state.</p>
  <p>Note that redaction is on by default: an agent reading flows sees masked sensitive headers and
    query values unless the policy is changed (see <a href="/en/docs/api#policy--redaction">API § policy</a>).</p>

  <h2>Use Cases</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>AI-powered API testing and debugging</li>
    <li>Automated traffic analysis</li>
    <li>Dynamic rule generation based on traffic patterns</li>
    <li>Integration with CI/CD pipelines</li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
